Subject: [PATCH] projectM TV: lines as quads that scale with the render size

projectM issue #682. MilkDrop draws waveforms, custom waves, shape outlines and motion vectors as
1 px GL lines (thick ones four times, offset by up to a pixel), so their share of the picture
changes with the render size and a preset that feeds its image back looked different at every
quality level. projectm_opengl_set_line_reference_size() draws them as quads instead: one instance
per segment with miter joins, 1 px wide at and below the reference size's area and widened by the
square root of the area ratio above it, so a line covers the same share of the picture at any size
and aspect ratio (at the reference's aspect ratio: the height ratio). Below the reference area,
thinner lines faded by their width made such presets go dark, so they are drawn as at the reference
size. Edges are hard like MilkDrop's GL lines (the quad is the band itself, so the rasterizer
resolves pixel-border ties; on GLES a 1/64 px offset makes those ties land on the row Mali's GL
lines light); projectm_opengl_set_line_antialiasing() opts into a one-pixel anti-aliased edge.
Thick lines are the thin line drawn four times with MilkDrop's offsets (a pixel for the main wave,
half a pixel for custom waves and shape outlines, scaled like the width). Waveform dots stay GL
points with a scaled size. If the driver rejects the line shaders, GL lines are drawn. 0, the
default, keeps the GL lines. Unlike the issue's suggestion, waveforms also get miter joins and flat
ends, not round joins and caps: round joins overlap the segments and blend joint pixels twice
(bright beads on translucent or additive waves), at 1-2 px the join shape is sub-pixel, and
MilkDrop's lines have none. MilkDrop's line waveforms also draw a third of their samples on renders
narrower than three times the sample count (160 instead of 480 dots below 1440 px), so the sample
count depends on the render size too; with a reference size the rule uses the reference-equivalent
width (render width / line scale), so the samples follow the reference like the line width (a 1080p
or 4K render with the 1024x768 reference draws MilkDrop's 160 dots, not 480, which washed out
feedback presets). Two more of MilkDrop's quantities tied to its render size follow the reference
size when lines are scaled (above the reference area), the size the presets were made for: the spiro
and hash waves' fade (Waveform::MaximizeColors(), by max(width, height)), and the blur levels, made as
from a source of the reference's size (the render size divided by the line scale) whose first pass
reads the previous frame's mipmaps (glGenerateMipmap per frame), so blur1-3 keep their size and their
radius keeps its share of the picture instead of shrinking with the render. Those mip levels stay
allocated, so the framebuffer texture pool (0007) counts a texture's whole mip chain
(Texture::StorageBytes()) against its limit and in projectm_opengl_texture_pool_bytes(). Preset code sees that
reference-sized canvas too (ShaderCanvasSize()): texsize and texsize_main, mip_x/y/avg and the
per-frame and per-pixel pixelsx/pixelsy report the render size divided by the line scale, as in
MilkDrop, where texsize is the internal canvas the preset was tuned on. Texel steps (texsize.zw * k)
and uv-to-pixel factors then keep their share of the picture: feedback presets that advect or
differentiate by a few texels per frame kept their pixel-sized steps at 4K and drifted 3.25x slower
(Acid Mandala: saturation 0.46 instead of 0.80). The textures, their sampling and the sizes of real
textures (texsize_<name>) are unchanged. Without a reference size, and at or below its area, all of
this is MilkDrop's behaviour, byte for byte.
---
diff --git a/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h b/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
index ee6ae6562..0262cc70a 100644
--- a/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
+++ b/src/api/include/projectM-4/render_opengl.h
@@ -87,6 +87,39 @@ PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_outgoing_preset_frame_divisor(projectm_
  */
 PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_direct_output(projectm_handle instance, bool enabled);
 
+/**
+ * @brief Draws waveforms, custom waves, shape outlines and motion vectors as quads, and waveform dots
+ *        with a matching point size, so they keep their share of the picture at large render sizes.
+ *
+ * Lines are 1 px wide at and below the given render size's area and widen by the square root of the
+ * area ratio above it, so a line covers the same share of the picture at any size and aspect ratio
+ * (at the reference's aspect ratio this is the render height over the reference height). Thick ones
+ * are drawn like MilkDrop's: the thin line four times with small offsets that scale the same way.
+ * Below the reference area lines are drawn as at it, because thinner, faded lines made presets that
+ * feed their image back go dark. Edges are hard, like MilkDrop's GL lines, unless
+ * projectm_opengl_set_line_antialiasing() is enabled. Dots remain GL points whose size scales the
+ * same way (on desktop OpenGL, GL_PROGRAM_POINT_SIZE is enabled while they are drawn). 0 in either
+ * dimension, the default, keeps MilkDrop's 1 px GL lines and points. If the driver rejects the line
+ * shaders, lines fall back to GL lines.
+ *
+ * @param instance The projectM instance handle.
+ * @param width Width of the reference render size in pixels, or 0.
+ * @param height Height of the reference render size in pixels, or 0.
+ */
+PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_line_reference_size(projectm_handle instance, uint32_t width, uint32_t height);
+
+/**
+ * @brief Gives quad lines (see projectm_opengl_set_line_reference_size()) a one-pixel anti-aliased edge.
+ *
+ * Off by default: quad lines have hard edges like MilkDrop's GL lines, which presets that feed their
+ * image back are tuned for (a soft edge made such a preset 14 % darker in testing). Has no effect
+ * while no line reference size is set, and none on dots.
+ *
+ * @param instance The projectM instance handle.
+ * @param enabled True for anti-aliased edges.
+ */
+PROJECTM_EXPORT void projectm_opengl_set_line_antialiasing(projectm_handle instance, bool enabled);
+
